WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.297
Consider a hypothetical professional discussion
service with tables for members, subscriptions and

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:06.683
posts.

00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:10.924
A member following forty authors requests the newest
twenty posts.

00:00:10.924 --> 00:00:17.221
A normalized design first finds the followed authors
and retrieves their recent posts, then orders

00:00:17.221 --> 00:00:18.635
and limits the result.

00:00:18.635 --> 00:00:22.491
This can work well with suitable indexes and modest
traffic.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.522
The architectural question is how much data and how
many storage owners the request must touch as

00:00:28.522 --> 00:00:29.826
the workload changes.

00:00:29.826 --> 00:00:35.103
Now compare a member who follows four thousand
authors spread across many partitions.

00:00:35.103 --> 00:00:41.062
The same logical query may require broader fan-out
and merging, even though the returned page is

00:00:41.062 --> 00:00:42.242
still twenty posts.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:45.623
A small result does not imply a small amount of
work.

00:00:45.623 --> 00:00:51.556
Measure feed-read frequency, author activity and
partition fan-out before changing the model.

00:00:51.556 --> 00:00:57.809
Normalization makes canonical updates
straightforward; it does not imply that every read
is cheap.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:02.530
Conversely, the presence of a join alone does not
prove the query is slow.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.719
The workload and access path provide the evidence.

00:01:05.742 --> 00:01:10.663
In the hypothetical discussion service, one author
has eight hundred followers.

00:01:10.663 --> 00:01:16.517
Materializing an entry in every follower's feed
turns one canonical post into as many as eight

00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:18.012
hundred derived updates.

00:01:18.012 --> 00:01:23.867
Readers benefit because their recent feed entries
are already grouped by their own identifier.

00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:29.971
At ten such posts per second, this illustrative case
creates up to eight thousand feed updates per

00:01:29.971 --> 00:01:32.214
second before retries or other work.

00:01:32.242 --> 00:01:35.959
Faster reads have purchased additional write
amplification.

00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:42.195
Queue the derived updates so the posting request can
acknowledge durable acceptance without waiting

00:01:42.195 --> 00:01:43.581
for every destination.

00:01:43.581 --> 00:01:49.503
Then define a freshness target for readers and
measure the oldest unfinished publication work.

00:01:49.503 --> 00:01:55.488
Workers can process bounded follower batches and use
stable post identifiers to avoid duplicate

00:01:55.488 --> 00:01:56.873
entries after retries.

00:01:56.873 --> 00:02:02.869
Keep the canonical post distinct from feed
references so that deletion or moderation has an
explicit

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:04.488
path through derived state.

00:02:04.488 --> 00:02:10.484
Asynchrony changes when the work happens and who
waits; it does not remove the work or automatically

00:02:10.484 --> 00:02:11.863
make all feeds current.
